WEBVTT

00:01.676 --> 00:02.808
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

00:02.810 --> 00:03.976
Thanks for coming in, guys.

00:03.978 --> 00:05.911
It's always
a pleasure seeing you.

00:05.913 --> 00:07.813
I mean, she's so pretty.
You're so pretty.

00:08.649 --> 00:10.883
You know anyone
we can set her up with?

00:10.885 --> 00:13.085
I appreciate the thought,
but I am perfectly happy

00:13.087 --> 00:15.221
with my... cats.

00:15.223 --> 00:17.223
Anyway...

00:17.225 --> 00:19.658
how would you guys feel
about adopting an older child?

00:19.660 --> 00:21.994
Wow. Like, how old
are we talking?

00:21.996 --> 00:24.563
Like, I've been taking care
of this one since he was 43.

00:25.566 --> 00:27.399
He's been a
wonderful provider.

00:27.401 --> 00:30.336
And he only spanks me
when I ask him to.

00:30.338 --> 00:34.139
Wow, that is a lot to take in
before 10:00 a.m. on a Monday.

00:34.141 --> 00:35.574
That's what he said.

00:35.576 --> 00:37.142
Okay, please stop.

00:37.144 --> 00:39.178
That's what he said.

00:39.180 --> 00:42.114
Oh! The reason I bring it up
is because there is

00:42.116 --> 00:44.016
a six-year-old boy
who's been bouncing around

00:44.018 --> 00:45.384
the foster system for a while,

00:45.386 --> 00:47.987
and I think he'd be
a great match for you guys.

00:47.989 --> 00:49.088
Wow, really?

00:49.090 --> 00:51.457
Uh, well, six
is a great age.

00:51.459 --> 00:53.893
Potty-trained
but not ashamed of you yet.

00:53.895 --> 00:55.294
Well...

00:55.296 --> 00:57.596
well, uh, can we adopt him
right away?

00:57.598 --> 00:59.164
Well, we'd start
with a weekend visit.

00:59.166 --> 01:01.500
See if it's a good fit,
then, assuming it is,

01:01.502 --> 01:03.402
you guys would become
his foster parents.

01:03.404 --> 01:05.437
After six months,
if everyone agrees,

01:05.439 --> 01:07.239
you can officially adopt him.

01:07.241 --> 01:09.275
Oh, and-and-and
if we don't agree,

01:09.277 --> 01:12.144
can we credit those six months
to a kid that we do like?

01:14.649 --> 01:17.216
Wow, I-I always thought
we were gonna get a baby,

01:17.218 --> 01:20.786
but I kind of like the idea
of giving an older kid a shot.

01:20.788 --> 01:23.222
Well, Louis certainly
deserves a shot.

01:23.224 --> 01:24.523
Louis?

01:24.525 --> 01:26.292
Louie?

01:26.294 --> 01:27.493
Aw, sweet Lou.

01:27.495 --> 01:29.128
Lou!

01:29.130 --> 01:31.597
Uh, is he married to that name
or can we change it?

01:31.599 --> 01:33.232
Alan.

01:33.234 --> 01:34.633
Oh, I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

01:34.635 --> 01:37.636
I just, you know,
I had my heart set on Beckett.

01:39.006 --> 01:40.773
Look, I'm gonna be
honest with you guys.

01:40.775 --> 01:42.408
Louis has had a rough time.

01:42.410 --> 01:44.376
He's been in foster homes,
group homes.

01:44.378 --> 01:45.844
He's moved around a lot.

01:45.846 --> 01:47.179
We thought we had him
placed for adoption

01:47.181 --> 01:48.614
a few months ago,
but it fell through.

01:48.616 --> 01:50.616
So he's gonna be
a little gun-shy.

01:50.618 --> 01:51.884
Well, don't worry about that.

01:51.886 --> 01:53.552
I mean, I'm sure we'll have
a great time together.

01:53.554 --> 01:54.787
Just don't expect a lot.

01:54.789 --> 01:57.256
Same thing I told this one
on our wedding night.

02:00.194 --> 02:02.494
Wow, I really like this idea.

02:02.496 --> 02:04.263
I mean, I'm gonna...
I'm gonna have a son.

02:04.265 --> 02:05.831
How cool is that gonna be?

02:05.833 --> 02:07.333
I mean, and-and
you get to relive

02:07.335 --> 02:09.835
all those great
father-son moments.

02:10.771 --> 02:14.039
♪ By the time
you hear the siren ♪

02:14.041 --> 02:16.475
♪ It's already too late ♪

02:16.477 --> 02:19.712
♪ One goes to the morgue
and the other to jail ♪

02:19.714 --> 02:21.413
♪ One gets wasted
and the other's a waste... ♪

02:22.216 --> 02:24.183
Good thinking.
Aah!

02:30.191 --> 02:31.624
Dad?
Yeah, buddy?

02:31.626 --> 02:34.893
Bite me.

02:40.735 --> 02:42.601
Yeah, all those

02:42.603 --> 02:45.537
great father-son moments.

02:45.539 --> 02:47.873
♪ Men, men, men, men, manly men,
men, men ♪

02:47.875 --> 02:49.554
♪ Ah. ♪
♪ Men. ♪

02:49.555 --> 02:53.555
&lt;font color="#00FF00">♪ Two and a Half Men 12x05 ♪&lt;/font>
&lt;font color="#00FFFF">Oontz Oontz Oontz&lt;/font>
Original Air Date on November 27, 2014

02:53.556 --> 02:58.556
== sync, corrected by &lt;font color=#00FF00>elderman&lt;/font> ==
&lt;font color=#00FFFF>@elder_man&lt;/font>

02:58.557 --> 02:59.957
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

03:01.627 --> 03:03.060
What the hell?

03:03.062 --> 03:04.861
- Walden?
- Up here!

03:04.863 --> 03:07.297
Check it out!
Isn't this awesome?

03:07.299 --> 03:09.366
They have one just like it
at the Google headquarters!

03:09.368 --> 03:11.234
Oh, you know what you get
when you Google

03:11.236 --> 03:13.103
“greatest dad in the world”?

03:14.607 --> 03:17.240
This guy!

03:18.877 --> 03:20.444
What is going on?

03:20.446 --> 03:21.511
What is all this?

03:21.513 --> 03:23.480
Oh, uh, well,
I went to the toy store

03:23.482 --> 03:24.848
and got a few things for Louis.

03:24.850 --> 03:27.317
“A few things”?
Okay, I don't want to be the guy

03:27.319 --> 03:29.186
to point out the 800-pound
gorilla in the room.

03:29.188 --> 03:31.989
But there's an 800-pound gorilla
in the room.

03:31.991 --> 03:33.924
I just want him to like it here.

03:33.926 --> 03:35.092
Well, so do I.

03:35.094 --> 03:37.060
But if you build Disneyland
in your house,

03:37.062 --> 03:40.030
you'll never get the thrill
of taking him to Disneyland.

03:40.032 --> 03:42.499
Ah. You're right.

03:42.501 --> 03:45.469
Damn it. I got to cancel
Mickey and Minnie.

03:45.471 --> 03:46.236
What?

03:46.238 --> 03:47.270
Oh, I was gonna have him

03:47.272 --> 03:48.405
wake him up in the morning.

03:48.407 --> 03:49.673
Hey, Louis.

03:49.675 --> 03:50.807
Ready for breakfast?

03:50.809 --> 03:52.009
We've got orange juice

03:52.011 --> 03:54.277
and waffles shaped just like me!

03:54.279 --> 03:57.014
I think it's great that you're
so excited about being a dad.

03:57.016 --> 04:00.651
And the best part is...
no stretch marks for me.

04:00.653 --> 04:03.186
You know, you're my best friend,

04:03.188 --> 04:05.389
but you're an odd little man.

04:07.026 --> 04:08.792
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

04:10.429 --> 04:11.662
Oh, hey.

04:11.664 --> 04:12.729
What are you doing up?

04:12.731 --> 04:14.064
I couldn't sleep.

04:14.066 --> 04:16.199
Oh, I get it.
Excited about tomorrow?

04:16.201 --> 04:18.602
More like terrified
about tomorrow.

04:19.438 --> 04:20.937
Why are you getting paper towels

04:20.939 --> 04:23.373
in the middle of the..
Oh, you... Never mind.

04:23.375 --> 04:24.808
I don't want to know.

04:24.810 --> 04:28.445
That's a good call.

04:29.248 --> 04:31.381
You want to talk about it?

04:31.383 --> 04:33.784
There's a kid
showing up here tomorrow.

04:33.786 --> 04:36.286
And he needs a dad,
and I don't know if I can do it.

04:36.288 --> 04:38.422
What? Why not?

04:38.424 --> 04:40.991
Well, I've never had
a successful relationship

04:40.993 --> 04:43.527
with anyone in my life
except for you.

04:43.529 --> 04:45.195
Four years next Thursday.

04:45.197 --> 04:47.097
Yeah, I got your wish list.

04:47.099 --> 04:50.167
They're just ideas.
Make it your own.

04:50.169 --> 04:52.269
Look, the fact that you've
screwed up with women

04:52.271 --> 04:53.704
doesn't mean you can't
be a great father.

04:53.706 --> 04:55.806
I don't even know
what a good dad looks like.

04:55.808 --> 04:57.340
I mean, I know
what a bad dad looks like.

04:57.342 --> 04:59.810
It's an empty chair
across the table from my mom.

04:59.812 --> 05:02.112
Which later gets filled
by my “Uncle Kenneth”

05:02.114 --> 05:03.447
who sold speedboats.

05:03.449 --> 05:06.249
And “Uncle Rob”
who sold insurance.

05:06.251 --> 05:08.418
Then “Uncle Stan” who sold pot.

05:08.420 --> 05:10.587
I liked Uncle Stan.

05:10.589 --> 05:12.689
I get it. I didn't grow up
with a dad, either.

05:12.691 --> 05:15.025
When Jake was born,
I was scared out of my mind.

05:15.027 --> 05:16.927
Why were you scared?
You're a great dad.

05:16.929 --> 05:20.263
Okay, I'm gonna
tell you something

05:20.265 --> 05:22.966
that I've, I've never
told anybody before.

05:22.968 --> 05:26.103
When Jake was a baby...

05:26.105 --> 05:28.171
I dropped him.

05:29.875 --> 05:32.743
Right smack on his head.

05:32.745 --> 05:35.312
Oh, my God.

05:35.314 --> 05:37.581
Now I feel awful
about all the times I said,

05:37.583 --> 05:41.184
“Were you dropped
on your head as a baby?”

05:41.186 --> 05:43.487
My point is
that Jake turned out fine.

05:43.489 --> 05:45.989
I mean, sure, he had to repeat
seventh grade math

05:45.991 --> 05:48.625
a couple of times, but he was
born at the right time.

05:48.627 --> 05:50.427
You know? Computers.

05:51.730 --> 05:53.697
Wait, so what you're saying is

05:53.699 --> 05:56.333
even if I screw up a little,
it's not the end of the world?

05:56.335 --> 05:58.535
The fact that you're worried
about being a good dad

05:58.537 --> 06:00.370
is what's gonna
make you a great one.

06:00.372 --> 06:02.973
Thanks.
Hmm.

06:02.975 --> 06:04.274
You're a good friend.

06:04.276 --> 06:05.876
All right.

06:05.878 --> 06:07.110
I'm gonna go get some sleep.

06:07.112 --> 06:09.412
Good night.
Good night.

06:13.685 --> 06:16.853
Oh, Baby Jake.

06:16.855 --> 06:18.889
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

06:18.891 --> 06:21.424
It'll be nice having
a kid around here again.

06:21.426 --> 06:23.493
Running up and
down the stairs.

06:23.495 --> 06:26.029
Laughing at all
the silliest things.

06:26.031 --> 06:29.199
And they'll believe almost
anything you tell them.

06:29.201 --> 06:30.467
No kidding.

06:30.469 --> 06:31.635
Jake still thinks
one of the first men

06:31.637 --> 06:32.769
on the moon was
Buzz Lightyear.

06:32.771 --> 06:33.870
Ha!

06:35.440 --> 06:37.207
I'll get it!

06:37.209 --> 06:40.644
You got to admit that would've
been way cooler with a slide.

06:46.218 --> 06:48.418
Look who's here.
Hi, guys.

06:48.420 --> 06:49.953
This is Louis.

06:49.955 --> 06:51.621
Hi, Louis!
Sweet Lou!

06:51.623 --> 06:54.324
Lou!

06:55.661 --> 06:57.594
We're not booing,
we're Lou-ing.

06:57.596 --> 07:01.264
Louis, this is Walden and Alan.

07:01.266 --> 07:02.299
Hi.

07:02.301 --> 07:03.301
You're gonna be staying

07:03.302 --> 07:04.301
with them for the weekend.

07:04.303 --> 07:05.869
Oh, uh, don't worry, buddy,

07:05.871 --> 07:08.371
I was only supposed
to stay here for a weekend, too.

07:08.373 --> 07:10.774
12 years later.

07:10.776 --> 07:12.909
Oh, and, uh,

07:12.911 --> 07:14.377
this is Berta.
Uh, she helps

07:14.379 --> 07:15.645
take care of us around here.

07:15.647 --> 07:17.614
Look at that face.

07:18.750 --> 07:21.351
Walden, you might have
some competition

07:21.353 --> 07:23.186
for the best looking
man in the house.

07:23.188 --> 07:25.088
You mean so-some
more competition?

07:25.090 --> 07:28.158
Nope.

07:28.160 --> 07:30.260
Do you have any Cheez-Its?

07:30.262 --> 07:33.630
Uh, well, if we don't,
we could get some.

07:33.632 --> 07:35.765
Okay, I'll stay.

07:35.767 --> 07:37.901
All right, well,
I want you guys

07:37.903 --> 07:39.603
to get to know each other,
so I'm gonna go.

07:39.605 --> 07:41.133
But you have my number
if you need anything.

07:41.157 --> 07:41.939
Oh, uh, wait.

07:41.940 --> 07:45.175
Uh, does-does he come with an
instruction manual or something?

07:45.177 --> 07:48.345
You'll be fine.
Bye, Louis.

07:48.347 --> 07:49.746
Uh, okay, uh...

07:49.748 --> 07:51.481
should we have tipped her?

07:51.483 --> 07:52.682
You're asking me?

07:52.684 --> 07:54.851
I don't even tip the pizza guy.

07:54.853 --> 07:56.353
I don't like pizza.

07:56.355 --> 07:57.854
I like Cheez-Its.

07:57.856 --> 08:00.090
Oh, well, you know,
when you think about it,

08:00.092 --> 08:03.260
a pizza is just
a really big Cheez-It

08:03.262 --> 08:05.662
with sauce.

08:06.765 --> 08:09.766
We're gonna get
Cheez-Its, right?

08:09.768 --> 08:11.167
Berta?

08:11.169 --> 08:12.302
I'm on it.

08:12.304 --> 08:14.771
I'll be back as
soon as I can.

08:14.773 --> 08:15.906
And, Louis?

08:15.908 --> 08:17.173
That's the first time I ever

08:17.175 --> 08:19.142
said that and meant it.

08:19.144 --> 08:21.711
Hey, Louis?
Welcome to our house.

08:21.713 --> 08:24.447
Do you want to come on in?

08:24.449 --> 08:25.882
Oh, my God. Look at him.

08:25.884 --> 08:27.183
He's adorable.

08:27.185 --> 08:29.653
I know, I know.
He's like one of those TV kids.

08:29.655 --> 08:30.921
Maybe we can put him
in commercials.

08:30.923 --> 08:32.455
We know he likes Cheez-Its.

08:32.457 --> 08:33.924
Alan, do you really want to be

08:33.926 --> 08:36.226
making less money
than a six-year-old?

08:36.228 --> 08:38.628
Oh, oh, oh. Oh.

08:38.630 --> 08:40.297
Who is that?

08:40.299 --> 08:41.464
This is Timothy.

08:41.466 --> 08:42.565
He's a seal.

08:42.567 --> 08:43.800
Oh! Well, hi, Timothy.

08:43.802 --> 08:44.868
I'm Walden.

08:44.870 --> 08:45.902
And I'm Alan.

08:45.904 --> 08:48.305
Timothy doesn't talk.

08:48.307 --> 08:49.606
Oh, right, yeah.

08:49.608 --> 08:51.308
Because he's a seal.

08:51.310 --> 08:54.010
No, because he's a toy.

08:54.012 --> 08:56.579
Right. He-He's a toy.

09:00.652 --> 09:04.020
Wow. Got a lot
of weekend left.

09:04.022 --> 09:07.924
So, which one of you
is the mommy?

09:08.593 --> 09:10.593
Um...
Um...

09:10.595 --> 09:12.896
well, that's-that's
a great question.

09:12.898 --> 09:14.264
And one I was hoping
you wouldn't ask

09:14.266 --> 09:15.832
in the first five minutes.

09:15.834 --> 09:17.233
You know, see, in some families,

09:17.235 --> 09:19.970
there's a mommy and a daddy.

09:19.972 --> 09:22.372
But in other families,
like this one,

09:22.374 --> 09:24.207
there's, there's two daddies.

09:24.209 --> 09:25.408
Why?

09:25.410 --> 09:30.013
Wow, that is
another great question.

09:30.015 --> 09:32.515
Um... Alan?

09:32.517 --> 09:34.284
No, no. You're doing great.

09:34.286 --> 09:35.418
Okay.

09:35.420 --> 09:37.320
He's the mommy.

09:37.322 --> 09:39.456
I told you, Timothy.

09:39.458 --> 09:41.758
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

09:48.402 --> 09:50.068
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

09:50.070 --> 09:52.804
Hey, Louis, check it out.

09:52.806 --> 09:55.607
Casa de Louis.

09:55.609 --> 09:57.276
Chez Louie.

09:57.278 --> 10:00.312
San Louis... Obispo.

10:01.915 --> 10:03.826
Basically what I'm saying is
this is your room.

10:03.826 --> 10:04.435
Cool!

10:04.459 --> 10:06.443
You know, I thought we could
decorate it together.

10:06.887 --> 10:09.655
Lucky. He never let me.

10:10.658 --> 10:12.024
Oh, and check this out.

10:12.026 --> 10:13.625
This is your bed.

10:13.627 --> 10:15.661
But if you're thinking about
jumping up and down on it,

10:15.663 --> 10:18.430
you're gonna be
in a lot of trouble...

10:18.432 --> 10:21.566
if you don't!

10:22.536 --> 10:25.003
Uh, we also heard, uh,
that you like to draw.

10:25.005 --> 10:27.372
So check out
this fully stocked art table.

10:27.374 --> 10:29.708
You got, uh, markers,
crayons, paints.

10:29.710 --> 10:33.445
Someone's gonna put
the “arty” back in “party.”

10:34.715 --> 10:37.049
Okay, so, uh, why don't we,
uh, unpack your stuff?

10:37.051 --> 10:39.551
We'll put it in the dresser,
and then we'll go do something.

10:39.553 --> 10:41.687
No, I don't need to unpack.

10:41.689 --> 10:42.988
Oh, okay.

10:42.990 --> 10:45.490
Uh, well, then it's straight
to the good times, huh?

10:45.492 --> 10:48.193
For King Louie
the Fun-teenth.

10:49.930 --> 10:51.596
I apologize.

10:51.598 --> 10:53.198
So, yeah,

10:53.200 --> 10:54.366
what do you want to do?

10:54.368 --> 10:55.867
You know, we could play a game,

10:55.869 --> 10:58.170
we could go to the park,
we could go to the zoo,

10:58.172 --> 11:00.405
we could go to SeaWorld
or Disneyland

11:00.407 --> 11:02.407
or Legoland, or we could
play Legos in the house,

11:02.409 --> 11:04.409
or we could play house,
or we could build a house,

11:04.411 --> 11:06.211
or we could listen
to house music.

11:06.213 --> 11:09.281
Oontz. Oontz. Oontz. Oontz.
Oontz. Oontz. Oontz. Oontz.

11:11.919 --> 11:13.485
What's up, Miami!

11:13.487 --> 11:14.720
Oontz. Oontz.

11:14.722 --> 11:18.523
Or-or we don't have
to do any of that.

11:18.525 --> 11:19.691
We could...

11:19.693 --> 11:22.494
do something else, like...

11:22.496 --> 11:23.695
Alan?

11:23.697 --> 11:25.564
Uh, uh, we could,
we could go to the beach.

11:25.566 --> 11:27.332
Oh, that's a great idea.

11:27.334 --> 11:30.569
Oh, but where are we
gonna find one of those?

11:30.571 --> 11:32.704
You live on the beach.

11:32.706 --> 11:34.773
Oh! That's right!

11:34.775 --> 11:36.908
It's your backyard.

11:36.910 --> 11:38.810
So, what do you say?
You want to go?

11:38.812 --> 11:39.878
Okay.

11:39.880 --> 11:41.980
We got an okay. Okay.
So let's go.

11:41.982 --> 11:43.749
Let's do this.
To the beach we go.

11:43.751 --> 11:46.451
Oh, hey, uh,
if you need a bathing suit,

11:46.453 --> 11:47.986
it's in the top drawer.

11:47.988 --> 11:50.322
So why don't you change,

11:50.324 --> 11:51.823
and we'll change,
and then we'll go

11:51.825 --> 11:53.759
have a party on the beach.

11:53.761 --> 11:56.762
Oontz. Oontz. Oontz. Oontz.
Oontz. Oontz. Oontz.

11:56.764 --> 11:58.597
Oontz. Oontz.
Oontz.

11:58.599 --> 11:59.564
Oontz. Oontz.

11:59.566 --> 12:01.967
Oh, my God,
that was like pulling teeth.

12:01.969 --> 12:03.802
Please tell me
this gets easier.

12:03.804 --> 12:04.903
It doesn't,

12:04.905 --> 12:06.972
but the good news
is, you stop trying.

12:06.974 --> 12:08.507
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

12:08.509 --> 12:10.475
Hey, Louis,
look at this.

12:10.477 --> 12:12.144
We're almost done.

12:12.146 --> 12:14.045
Yes, now all we need

12:14.047 --> 12:15.414
is a flag for the top.
Oh!

12:15.416 --> 12:17.449
Oh, that's an old Band-Aid.

12:17.451 --> 12:18.984
Uh, how about this?

12:18.986 --> 12:21.119
We'll take this juice box,

12:21.121 --> 12:23.855
flip it up here, and we'll put
the straw in like this,

12:23.857 --> 12:25.090
and ta-da!

12:25.092 --> 12:26.425
It's a flag!

12:26.427 --> 12:27.859
Look at this, Louis!

12:27.861 --> 12:29.194
All hail

12:29.196 --> 12:32.631
the Kingdom
of Mount Berry Cooler!

12:32.633 --> 12:34.599
Oh, oh,

12:34.601 --> 12:37.369
and here comes the king, uh,
returning home from battle.

12:37.371 --> 12:40.305
And what does
he have with him?

12:40.307 --> 12:41.673
The spoils of war,

12:41.675 --> 12:45.410
all the Cheez-Its in the land.

12:45.412 --> 12:47.179
Isn't this cool, Louis?

12:47.181 --> 12:48.880
I want to go back inside.

12:48.882 --> 12:50.015
What?

12:50.017 --> 12:51.116
It's kind of hot,

12:51.118 --> 12:52.684
and I have to pee.

12:52.686 --> 12:53.919
Well, uh, you know,

12:53.921 --> 12:56.321
the ocean solves
both of those problems.

12:56.323 --> 12:58.757
O-O-Okay.

12:58.759 --> 13:01.393
Okay,
w-w-we'll meet you inside.

13:01.395 --> 13:03.228
Hey, hey, Berta,
he's coming in!

13:03.230 --> 13:04.496
I've got him!

13:04.498 --> 13:07.532
Well, he hates me.

13:07.534 --> 13:08.600
What?

13:08.602 --> 13:10.202
We spent an hour

13:10.204 --> 13:11.570
building a sand castle,

13:11.572 --> 13:13.238
and all he did was dig a hole.

13:13.240 --> 13:14.506
Who digs holes, Alan?

13:15.442 --> 13:17.409
Prisoners, they want to escape.

13:17.411 --> 13:19.377
Oh, Walden.

13:19.379 --> 13:22.147
Why are the pretty ones
always so crazy?

13:22.149 --> 13:23.748
Crazy? Am I?

13:23.750 --> 13:25.951
Really? What about earlier
when he didn't want to unpack?

13:25.953 --> 13:27.052
What was that all about?

13:27.054 --> 13:28.920
Nobody likes to unpack.
I hate it.

13:28.922 --> 13:30.755
Oh, is that why
you've never moved out?

13:30.757 --> 13:32.023
Wow.

13:32.025 --> 13:33.959
Um, misdirected anger,

13:33.961 --> 13:35.494
your table is ready.

13:35.496 --> 13:37.929
You know what,
he doesn't want to be here.

13:37.931 --> 13:39.331
That's it.

13:39.333 --> 13:41.166
I-I knew I couldn't do this.

13:41.168 --> 13:43.134
Come on, Walden,
put yourself in his shoes.

13:43.136 --> 13:45.670
He only spent
half a day with us.

13:45.672 --> 13:48.206
He's gonna need more time
to feel safe here.

13:48.208 --> 13:49.941
Yeah, right.

13:49.943 --> 13:52.110
More time.

13:53.413 --> 13:56.181
You think he'll love me
by tomorrow?

13:56.183 --> 13:57.415
Trust me, Walden,

13:57.417 --> 13:59.351
he'll warm up to you
just like I did.

13:59.353 --> 14:00.585
Remember when you moved here?

14:00.587 --> 14:02.420
I wasn't sure
I wanted you to stay.

14:02.422 --> 14:03.889
I bought the house.

14:03.891 --> 14:05.757
And I made it a home.

14:06.793 --> 14:08.593
You're gonna have
to have more patience with him.

14:08.595 --> 14:11.363
Fine.

14:11.365 --> 14:12.998
Patience.

14:14.601 --> 14:16.134
Where you going?
The ocean.

14:16.136 --> 14:18.136
I'm hot and I got to pee.

14:18.138 --> 14:20.739
Well, be careful.

14:21.508 --> 14:23.708
He hates me.

14:27.414 --> 14:29.347
Oh, there he is.

14:29.349 --> 14:31.216
Did you put the seat down?

14:31.218 --> 14:32.984
I never put it up.

14:35.055 --> 14:36.421
Can I have a snack?

14:36.423 --> 14:38.323
Do we have snacks?

14:39.493 --> 14:41.693
Let's see.

14:45.432 --> 14:47.299
Wow.

14:47.301 --> 14:49.467
Take a load off.

14:52.739 --> 14:54.773
When a guy named Charlie
lived here,

14:54.775 --> 14:57.809
I had to keep this cabinet

14:57.811 --> 15:00.645
stocked with coke...

15:01.815 --> 15:04.449
...co powder.

15:04.451 --> 15:07.452
Because he loved hot chocolate.

15:07.454 --> 15:09.220
I like hot chocolate.

15:09.222 --> 15:11.222
Not like he did.

15:11.224 --> 15:14.025
You like your crackers?

15:14.027 --> 15:15.126
Yeah.

15:15.128 --> 15:16.494
You don't talk much, do you?

15:18.098 --> 15:19.364
I get it.

15:19.366 --> 15:20.966
I was a quiet child.

15:20.968 --> 15:22.567
In fact,

15:22.569 --> 15:24.669
you and I have a lot in common.

15:24.671 --> 15:26.705
You like Cheez-Its, too?

15:26.707 --> 15:28.440
I do.

15:28.442 --> 15:30.775
But I was also raised

15:30.777 --> 15:32.811
in a whole bunch
of foster homes.

15:32.813 --> 15:34.045
You were?

15:34.047 --> 15:36.214
Yep, and I hated it.

15:36.216 --> 15:38.950
Moving around
all the time,

15:38.952 --> 15:41.186
having to make friends
everywhere,

15:41.188 --> 15:44.589
and no one ever offered me
grape juice.

15:46.159 --> 15:48.526
Would you like to have
some grape juice?

15:49.363 --> 15:50.762
Yes, please.

15:50.764 --> 15:52.697
But I got to tell you,

15:52.699 --> 15:55.900
we both hit the lottery
with this place.

15:55.902 --> 15:58.303
Walden and Alan

15:58.305 --> 16:00.238
are the best.

16:00.240 --> 16:02.040
They're nice,

16:02.042 --> 16:04.576
they're fun,

16:04.578 --> 16:06.211
and guess what else.

16:06.213 --> 16:07.312
What?

16:07.314 --> 16:09.514
They're easy to fool.

16:11.284 --> 16:14.753
Last year, I went to
my granddaughter's graduation

16:14.755 --> 16:16.087
seven times.

16:18.392 --> 16:19.724
And with that face...

16:20.661 --> 16:23.028
...you can get anything
you want.

16:24.131 --> 16:25.664
Welcome to Malibu, kid.

16:27.267 --> 16:29.000
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

16:30.904 --> 16:32.937
Can I come in?

16:34.241 --> 16:36.307
What you drawing?

16:36.309 --> 16:38.143
Oh.

16:38.145 --> 16:39.744
A sand castle.

16:39.746 --> 16:41.980
That's ironic.

16:41.982 --> 16:44.182
Hey, uh, c-can we
talk for a second?

16:44.184 --> 16:45.917
Sure.
All right.

16:45.919 --> 16:47.085
Great.

16:47.087 --> 16:48.420
Look, uh...

16:50.023 --> 16:52.357
I know it's got
to be overwhelming being here

16:52.359 --> 16:55.393
and being in new places
all the time

16:55.395 --> 16:58.029
and with new people
and a new bed.

16:58.031 --> 16:59.164
I mean, heck, for all you know,

16:59.166 --> 17:00.665
there could be monsters
under the bed.

17:00.667 --> 17:02.600
There's monsters?
No.

17:02.602 --> 17:04.035
That's a bad example.

17:04.037 --> 17:06.638
In fact, before you got here,

17:06.640 --> 17:09.174
I de-monsterized
the entire house.

17:11.378 --> 17:13.845
My point is, is...

17:13.847 --> 17:15.647
I'm really glad
you're here, Louis.

17:16.783 --> 17:19.050
I just want you
to be comfortable

17:19.052 --> 17:20.885
with Alan and I.

17:20.887 --> 17:23.321
So if, so if there's anything
you ever need to talk about,

17:23.323 --> 17:25.657
like anything at all,
you can come to me.

17:27.461 --> 17:29.661
So, that's all I have to say.

17:32.365 --> 17:33.932
Is there anything
you would like to say?

17:33.934 --> 17:35.467
Nope.

17:35.469 --> 17:37.035
Really.

17:37.037 --> 17:38.737
Just, like, just nothing?

17:38.739 --> 17:41.106
Like, nothing at all?

17:41.108 --> 17:42.807
Can I ask you a question?

17:42.809 --> 17:44.075
Uh, yes, of course.

17:44.077 --> 17:45.243
You can just, any time.

17:45.245 --> 17:46.344
That-That's why I'm here.

17:46.346 --> 17:47.612
So you just ask away.

17:47.614 --> 17:51.282
Is it okay that I can draw
by myself for a little while?

17:54.187 --> 17:56.254
Yeah.

17:56.256 --> 17:57.889
Okay, yeah.

17:57.891 --> 18:00.024
You-you want to be alone.

18:00.026 --> 18:01.392
I get that.

18:01.394 --> 18:03.294
That is not a problem.

18:03.296 --> 18:06.397
So, you want your space,

18:06.399 --> 18:08.900
and I shall give it to you.

18:08.902 --> 18:10.869
I will withdraw while you draw.

18:17.144 --> 18:19.310
Son of a bitch.

18:22.315 --> 18:24.616
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

18:28.622 --> 18:29.687
Walden?

18:29.689 --> 18:31.189
Oh, God.

18:31.191 --> 18:33.191
Oh, Louis.

18:33.193 --> 18:34.826
Oh, hey.

18:36.296 --> 18:37.829
What's going on, man?

18:37.831 --> 18:39.130
It's really late.

18:39.132 --> 18:41.466
I had a question, and you said
we could talk any time.

18:41.468 --> 18:43.168
Well, that's-that's just

18:43.170 --> 18:44.302
something that people say,

18:44.304 --> 18:45.770
but, I, uh...

18:45.772 --> 18:48.373
Yeah, what-what's up?

18:48.375 --> 18:50.074
Am I gonna stay here with you?

18:50.076 --> 18:51.476
What do you mean?

18:51.478 --> 18:53.611
Sometimes I go to a house
for a weekend,

18:53.613 --> 18:54.946
but then I don't stay.

18:54.948 --> 18:57.248
Oh, buddy, that's what
you're worried about.

18:57.250 --> 18:59.450
Well, come here,
c-come here, okay?

19:00.253 --> 19:02.153
Jump up here.

19:03.190 --> 19:04.455
Hey,

19:04.457 --> 19:05.657
listen.

19:05.659 --> 19:08.960
You can stay here as long
as you want to.

19:08.962 --> 19:10.862
Do you want to?

19:10.864 --> 19:13.898
Uh-huh. I really like it here.

19:13.900 --> 19:15.967
That's... great.

19:15.969 --> 19:18.503
Really surprising,
but it's great.

19:19.339 --> 19:20.471
Hey,

19:20.473 --> 19:21.940
I'm not going anywhere,

19:21.942 --> 19:24.475
and as long as you want
to be here,

19:24.477 --> 19:25.944
you're not going anywhere,
either.

19:25.946 --> 19:30.081
And believe me, Alan is gonna be
here longer than the both of us.

19:31.151 --> 19:32.817
So don't you worry.

19:32.819 --> 19:34.586
Thanks.

19:34.588 --> 19:35.887
So you really like it here, huh?

19:35.889 --> 19:38.523
All right, what was
the best part of the day?

19:38.525 --> 19:39.858
The Cheez-Its.

19:39.860 --> 19:42.260
Yeah.

19:42.262 --> 19:43.494
And I like Berta.

19:43.496 --> 19:44.963
Berta's great,

19:44.965 --> 19:46.297
and you like me, too, right?

19:46.299 --> 19:48.433
Berta's so funny.

19:48.435 --> 19:50.368
Patience, patience.

19:50.370 --> 19:52.003
Listen,

19:52.005 --> 19:52.937
as long as you're happy.

19:52.939 --> 19:54.472
Cool.

19:54.474 --> 19:56.975
It is cool, Louis.

19:56.977 --> 19:58.643
It's very cool.

20:01.448 --> 20:03.281
Where's Alan?

20:06.219 --> 20:08.152
Look at us.

20:08.154 --> 20:09.821
We're like
the Three Musketeers.

20:09.823 --> 20:11.489
I don't think the
Three Musketeers

20:11.491 --> 20:13.157
shared a bed.

20:13.159 --> 20:14.325
You don't know that.

20:14.327 --> 20:15.927
They were French.

20:15.929 --> 20:17.495
Where's Berta?

20:17.497 --> 20:18.630
No.

20:18.632 --> 20:20.932
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

20:26.340 --> 20:27.539
♪&lt;i> Men.&lt;/i> ♪

20:27.541 --> 20:29.311
And then you pull the bird back,
Uh-huh.

20:29.312 --> 20:31.925
and then let them go fly
into the pig's house.

20:32.153 --> 20:35.888
And then they roll around,
and then they explode.

20:35.890 --> 20:37.690
And then it's fun.

20:37.692 --> 20:39.425
Angry Birds sounds like
it's fun.

20:39.427 --> 20:42.094
You know,
I bet the birds are so angry

20:42.096 --> 20:44.029
'cause they don't get
enough sleep.

20:44.031 --> 20:46.165
It's the pigs
who should be angry.

20:46.167 --> 20:48.701
The birds are always crashing
into their house,

20:48.703 --> 20:51.904
even when they build them
out of ice or in space.

20:51.906 --> 20:54.202
I wonder if the pigs are
from Three Little Pigs.

20:54.527 --> 20:56.394
That's messed up
because the wolf already broke

20:56.396 --> 20:58.555
their old houses.

20:58.556 --> 21:00.049
Little help, Alan?

21:03.017 --> 21:09.517
== sync, corrected by &lt;font color=#00FF00>elderman&lt;/font> ==
&lt;font color=#00FFFF>@elder_man&lt;/font>
